Aligned on D.9a.

I think this resolves the objective issue more cleanly than either of the previous positions.

I agree that the real distinction is not:

- objective exists
vs
- objective does not exist

but rather:

- objective is determined by the input
vs
- objective is not determined by the input

The completion-test criterion also feels much more auditable than treating objective as a special render case.

The examples:

- summarize this report
- write an SOP for month-end close
- build an FAQ page about returns

all feel correctly determined.

While:

- create a dashboard
- write an SOP
- build an AI financial advisor

correctly remain open because the missing information is still inside the artefact definition itself.

I also agree with the restriction that objective population may not introduce new nouns.

That feels like one of the cleaner anti-invention safeguards added during this review.

[N/A] cap also looks good.

One UX request before consolidation:

For the Type 2 example banks, I would like the rendering to use one example per line rather than an inline sentence.

Current:

Examples of the kind of answer:
businesses · internal employees · retail investors · students

Preferred:

Examples of the kind of answer:

• businesses
• internal employees
• retail investors
• students

or equivalent vertical formatting.

My reasoning is purely readability.

These are no longer options, defaults, or menu selections, so I'm not concerned about the visual appearance of a list.

A vertical layout is much easier to scan, especially once the examples become slightly longer.

Similarly for the other banks:

Examples of the kind of answer:

• a decision is made
• a document exists
• a process runs without you
• someone's behaviour changes

I think the readability gain outweighs any risk of users interpreting them as selectable options, since the surrounding wording already makes their purpose explicit.

Aside from that, I think the architecture is effectively closed.

For the remaining items:

1. I will confirm the final definition-slot list against the live spec.

2. I do not currently expect echo-rate sampling to have an owner. Unless there is someone actually responsible for monitoring it, I would rather remove the threshold from the spec and keep only the structural safeguards:
   - fixed banks
   - generic examples
   - spanning examples
   - out-of-domain examples

A metric without an owner risks looking like a guarantee when it is not being actively reviewed.

With those adjustments, please proceed with the full consolidated specification.
